{#
  Deduplication check for the distillation job.

  Called once per proposed-new hypothesis, BEFORE inserting it.
  If an existing hypothesis is substantively the same, return its ID so
  the distillation job treats the evidence as match_existing instead of
  creating a duplicate.

  Keep the call cheap: small payload, yes/no output.
#}
You are checking whether a proposed new hypothesis about a user is substantively
the same as any existing hypothesis.

Proposed hypothesis:
"{{ proposed_statement }}"

Existing hypotheses:
{% if existing_hypotheses %}
{% for h in existing_hypotheses %}
- id: {{ h.hypothesis_id }}
  statement: "{{ h.statement }}"
  confidence: {{ h.confidence }}
{% endfor %}
{% else %}
(none)
{% endif %}

Task: decide whether the proposed hypothesis expresses the SAME underlying
trait, pattern, or value as any existing hypothesis.

Two hypotheses are the SAME if they would be updated by the same evidence
and describe the same aspect of the user. Minor wording differences do NOT
make them different. A hypothesis that is a narrower or broader version of
an existing one counts as the same.

Two hypotheses are DIFFERENT if they describe genuinely distinct traits that
would NOT always move together (e.g., "prefers solo work" vs "dislikes
bureaucracy" are different even if related).

Respond with JSON only — no prose, no markdown fences:

If a match exists:
{"duplicate": true, "existing_id": "<the matching hypothesis_id>", "reason": "<one sentence>"}

If no match:
{"duplicate": false, "existing_id": null, "reason": "<one sentence>"}
